      "severity": "medium",
      "expected_category": "other",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "manual_review",
        "cve_id": null,
        "description": "Resembles weak password hashing: a function literally named hash_secret applies a single unsalted hashlib.sha256(secret).hexdigest(), which scanners flag as CWE-916/328 (fast/unsalted hash for credentials, no PBKDF2/bcrypt/argon2). It is actually safe because the input is NOT a user password: APIKey.issue/rotate (lines 421/433) generate the secret as f\"coc_{secrets.token_urlsafe(32)}\" \u2014 a 256-bit cryptographically-random token. For high-entropy random API keys, a plain SHA-256 lookup hash is the correct, recommended construction; salting and key-stretching exist to defend low-entropy human passwords against brute force, which does not apply here. Distinct from the partner_console md5/sha1 weak-hash entry at line 95."
      },
